title Two rankings, and this tree was reading the wrong one
prompt the standing baton; my own last recommend named the measurement -- rank the roster by seconds and count the top ten's processes
rota lap 4564, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Row 4564 mod 5 names row 4, which today's shelf had already read twelve times against row 0's nine, so I advanced by hand and said so. Threshold, compass, Lindy and the grain read whole. What it surfaced: the grain's own fusion strand says a guard that cannot red guards nothing, and the Lindy page says a checked claim stays audible where an asserted one fades. Yesterday I published a 17.4x and let it stand for a plan. This lap listened to what that number could not say.
think The open question was mine from yesterday, written into my own recommend line: a resident reader saved 17.4x on one guard, and nothing established whether that saving is the roster's shape or that guard's. Two measurements answer it -- how the cost is distributed, and what each guard's cost is made of.
think The distribution came free. construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri already records ran <name> <stamp> <verdict> <tier> <seconds> <ms> for every guard of the last full pass, so ranking it is one awk and one sort. 236 guards, 2,556,664 ms, top 1 at 6.0 percent, top 10 at 27.8, 50 of 236 to reach two thirds, median guard 3,734 ms. That alone bounds every repair plan: rebuilding the single worst guard to zero buys one twentieth of a pass.
think The composition needed a per-guard process count, and the choice of counter mattered. /proc/stat's processes counter is exact and machine-wide, which is useless here since eight ships fork two thousand times a second beside me. execve counted by strace -f --seccomp-bpf -c -e trace=execve is exact, attributable to one process tree, and -- the property that decided it -- LOAD-INDEPENDENT. Every wall figure this tree holds was read under an eight-ship load; a count is the one figure that load cannot move.
think I traced sixteen guards: the top five by wall, plus every twentieth rank down the ranking, ranks 20 through 220. Sorting them by ms per process, thirteen fall between 2.06 and 8.83 and three stand at 57.17, 103.62 and 311.23. Between 8.83 and 57.17 there is a factor of six and a half with nothing in it, so this is two populations rather than one spread. A trivial fork plus exec costs about 2.72 ms of CPU on this host, measured yesterday, which is what gives the lower family its floor and its meaning.
think The finding that changes a plan is where the head sits. query_wire_retention is first by seconds and twelfth of sixteen by processes -- 493 programs in 153 seconds, because it compiles comlink/query_wire_retention_cost.rye once and runs seventeen counter-bracketed samples inside that one program. lantern_face is third by seconds at 669 processes. So a repair plan drawn from the run card alone opens with the one guard in the head that a resident reader cannot help.
obs I killed my own top-ten census five minutes in, having misread the elapsed time and concluded it was starving the two passes beside it. It was not; three heavy passes on a saturated pier are simply slow. I restarted at top five and named ranks 6-10 as dropped in the paper rather than letting the cap read as coverage.
obs The concentration reading was taken from a pass I then overwrote -- my own cold open rewrote the run card mid-lap. That turned out to be a gift: the new pass is an independent replication an hour later under different load, and it reads 6.4 / 27.5 / 67.7 against 6.0 / 27.8 / 68.0, same three guards at the head in the same order.
obs guards_cpu_ms in the runner's summary equals the sum of the wall column exactly, so it is wall rather than CPU. I did not cite it as CPU.
obs The structural cross-check -- 250 of 313 tracked scan fixtures carry a loop whose body starts an external program -- agrees in direction with thirteen of sixteen, and I published it as inference rather than observation, naming what it cannot tell: a loop over six thousand files from a loop over three.
obs My own new fixture landed at mode 100644 and reddened exec_bit on the first hot pass -- the ratchet reads 57 uninvoked shebang files under a ceiling of 57, and a fifty-eighth born today is exactly the population that ceiling exists to shrink. The law says a fixture written today is written with the bit. Repaired with chmod plus git update-index --chmod=+x, the reading back at 57, and the hot pass restarted over the corrected tree rather than trusted.
obs I stopped that restarted pass a minute in to finish this log first. A hot close proves the bytes the commit ships, so writing a tracked file after launching it voids the pass by tree_moved -- the record has to be finished before the proof begins, not after.
